The problem asks us to simulate a process involving $N$ aliens.
There are $N$ aliens, indexed $1$ to $N$.
The $i$-th alien starts with $A_i$ stones.
The $i$-th alien becomes an adult exactly $i$ years later.
When an alien becomes an adult (at year $i$), every *adult* who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone to the newly adult alien.
We need to find the number of stones each alien has after $N$ years.
The input constraints are $N \le 5 \times 10^5$ and $A_i \le 5 \times 10^5$.

Let's trace the process carefully.
At year $t$ (where $t$ ranges from 1 to $N$), the $t$-th alien becomes an adult.
Before year $t$, aliens $1, 2, \dots, t-1$ have already become adults.
Aliens $t+1, \dots, N$ are still minors.
The $t$-th alien becomes an adult.
The rule says: "every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone as a congratulatory gift to the alien who has just become an adult."
Who are the adults at year $t$?
The problem statement says "When someone becomes an adult... every adult who has at least one stone gives...".
Usually, "adults" refers to those who have already become adults.
Let's check the sample explanation to clarify.
Sample 1: $N=4$, $A = [5, 0, 9, 3]$.
Initially (Year 0): $C_1=5, C_2=0, C_3=9, C_4=3$.
After 1 year (Year 1): Alien 1 becomes adult.
Adults present: Alien 1 (just became adult).
Wait, does Alien 1 give stones to itself? Or does it receive stones?
The text says "gives exactly one stone ... to the alien who has just become an adult".
If Alien 1 is the one becoming adult, who are the other adults? None.
So Alien 1 receives 0 stones.
