This means we are counting pairs of indices $(i, j)$ such that $A_i$ is placed on $A_j$.
Since $A$ is placed on $B$, the condition is $A_i \le \frac{A_j}{2}$.
Also, usually, a kagamimochi is formed by two distinct mochi? The problem says "You choose two mochi out of the N mochi". It doesn't explicitly say distinct indices, but physically you can't place a mochi on itself. However, the condition $A_i \le \frac{A_j}{2}$ implies $A_i \le A_j/2 < A_j$ (since $A_j \ge 1$), so $A_i < A_j$. Thus, $A_i$ and $A_j$ must be different sizes, and since the array is sorted, $i < j$.
