The problem asks us to calculate the sum of $f(P)$ over all permutations $P$ of the sequence $(1, 2, \ldots, N)$, modulo 998244353.
Here, $f(P)$ is formed by concatenating the decimal representations of the elements of $P$ and interpreting the result as an integer.

Let $P = (P_1, P_2, \ldots, P_N)$ be a permutation of $(1, 2, \ldots, N)$.
$f(P)$ is the number formed by concatenating $P_1, P_2, \ldots, P_N$.
Let $L_i$ be the number of digits in the decimal representation of $i$.
Then $f(P)$ can be written as:
$f(P) = \sum_{i=1}^N P_i \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$
where $L_x$ is the number of digits of $x$.

We need to compute $\sum_{P} f(P) \pmod{998244353}$.
By linearity of expectation (or just summing over terms),
$\sum_{P} f(P) = \sum_{P} \sum_{i=1}^N P_i \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$
$= \sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{P} P_i \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$

Let's fix a position $k$ in the permutation (from 1 to $N$). Let the element at position $k$ be $x$.
The contribution of $x$ to the sum is $x \times 10^{\text{shift}}$, where $\text{shift}$ is the total number of digits contributed by elements placed at positions $k+1, \ldots, N$.
Since we are summing over all permutations, we can iterate over which element $x$ is placed at position $k$.
For a fixed position $k$ and a fixed value $x \in \{1, \ldots, N\}$, how many permutations have $P_k = x$?
There are $(N-1)!$ such permutations.
For these permutations, the elements at positions $k+1, \ldots, N$ are a permutation of the set $\{1, \ldots, N\} \setminus \{x\}$.
The exponent of 10 depends on the number of digits of the elements at positions $k+1, \ldots, N$.
Let $S_{k+1, \ldots, N}$ be the set of elements at positions $k+1, \ldots, N$.
The exponent is $\sum_{y \in S_{k+1, \ldots, N}} L_y$.
Since $S_{k+1, \ldots, N}$ is just the set of all elements except $x$, the sum of digits is constant regardless of the specific permutation of the remaining elements.
Let $TotalDigits = \sum_{y=1}^N L_y$.
Then the exponent for $x$ at position $k$ is $TotalDigits - L_x$.
So, for a fixed $k$ and fixed $x$, the term is $x \times 10^{TotalDigits - L_x}$.
This term appears in $(N-1)!$ permutations.
So the total sum is:
$\sum_{k=1}^N \sum_{x=1}^N (N-1)! \times x \times 10^{TotalDigits - L_x}$
$= (N-1)! \sum_{k=1}^N \sum_{x=1}^N x \times 10^{TotalDigits - L_x}$
Notice that the inner sum $\sum_{x=1}^N x \times 10^{TotalDigits - L_x}$ does not depend on $k$.
So we have:
$= (N-1)! \times N \times \sum_{x=1}^N x \times 10^{TotalDigits - L_x}$
$= N! \sum_{x=1}^N x \times 10^{TotalDigits - L_x}$

